1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,060
you

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,060
you

3
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:41,570
Krishnamurti in dialogue with Dr. Alan

4
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:45,080
W. Anderson. J. Krishnamurti was born in

5
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:47,680
South India and educated in England. For

6
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:50,280
the past 40 years he has been speaking

7
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:52,760
in the United States, Europe, India,

8
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:55,240
Australia, and other parts of the world.

9
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:57,620
From the outset of his life's

10
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:00,000
work he repudiated all connections with

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,480
organized religions and ideologies and said

12
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:04,959
that his only concern was to set

13
00:01:04,959 --> 00:01:08,080
men absolutely unconditionally free. He is

14
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:11,200
the author of many books, among them

15
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:14,020
The Awakening of Intelligence, The Urgency

16
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:16,840
of Change, Freedom from the Known, and

17
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:20,120
The Flight of the Eagle. This is

18
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:23,400
one of a series of dialogues between Krishnamurti

19
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:25,520
and Dr. Alan W. Anderson, who

20
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:27,640
is professor of religious studies at San

21
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:29,820
Diego State University where he teaches

22
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:32,000
Indian and Chinese scriptures and the

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,020
oracular tradition. Dr. Anderson, a

24
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:38,040
published poet, received his degree from

25
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:40,380
Columbia University and the Union

26
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:42,720
Theological Seminary. He has been honored

27
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:45,720
with a Distinguished Teaching Award

28
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:48,720
from the California State Universities.

29
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:53,480
Mr. Krishnamurti, in our last

30
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:57,480
conversation together we had moved from

31
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:02,460
speaking together concerning fear and

32
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:07,440
the relation between that and

33
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:10,539
transformation of the individual person which

34
00:02:10,539 --> 00:02:13,640
is not dependent on knowledge or

35
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:16,340
time and then from that we went to

36
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:19,040
pleasure and just as we reached the end

37
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:22,359
of the conversation the question of

38
00:02:22,359 --> 00:02:25,679
beauty arose and if it's agreeable with

39
00:02:25,679 --> 00:02:29,019
you I should like very much

40
00:02:29,019 --> 00:02:32,359
for us to explore that together.

41
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:39,360
One often wonders why museums are

42
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:45,360
so filled with pictures and statues.

43
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:49,860
Is it because man has lost touch with

44
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:53,360
nature and therefore has to go to these

45
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:56,860
museums to look at other people's

46
00:02:56,860 --> 00:03:00,360
paintings, famous paintings, and some of

47
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:05,360
them are really marvellously beautiful. Why

48
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:10,360
do the museums exist at all?

49
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:14,860
I'm just asking, I'm not saying they

50
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:18,360
should or should not. And I've been to

51
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:26,360
many museums all over the world, taken around by experts.

52
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:35,860
And I've always felt as

53
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:41,360
though I was being shown around

54
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:47,860
and looking at things that were

55
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:52,360
so, for me, artificial, so other people's

56
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:56,860
expression, what they consider beauty,

57
00:03:56,860 --> 00:04:01,360
and I wondered what is beauty?

58
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:06,860
Because when you read a poem of Keats

59
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:12,360
or some really a poem that a man writes

60
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:16,860
with his heart and with very deep

61
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:21,360
feeling, he wants to convey something to

62
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:26,360
you of what he feels, what he

63
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:31,360
considers to be the most exquisite essence of

64
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:37,860
beauty. And I've looked at great many

65
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:44,360
cathedrals, as you must have, over Europe,

66
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:49,860
and again this expression of

67
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:55,360
their feelings, their devotion, their reverence

68
00:04:55,360 --> 00:05:02,360
to in masonry, in rock, in buildings,

69
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:09,360
in marvellous cathedrals, and looking at all

70
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:14,860
this, I'm always surprised when people

71
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:20,360
talk about beauty or write about beauty

72
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:27,360
whether it is something created by man or

73
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:34,360
something that you see in nature, or it has

74
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:41,360
nothing to do with the stone or with

75
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:48,360
the paint or with the word, but something deeply

76
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:56,360
ingood. And so, often in discussing

77
00:05:56,360 --> 00:06:04,360
with so-called professionals, having a dialogue

78
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:11,360
with them, it appears to me that it

79
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:18,360
is always somewhere out there, the modern painting,

80
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:23,860
modern music, the pop, and so on,

81
00:06:23,860 --> 00:06:29,360
so on, it's always somehow so dreadfully artificial.

82
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:36,860
I may be wrong, but so what is

83
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:44,360
beauty? Must it be expressed? That's one question.

84
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:51,860
Does it need the word, the stone, the

85
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:59,360
colour, the paint, or it is something that cannot

86
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:05,860
possibly be expressed in word, in a building, in

87
00:07:05,860 --> 00:07:12,360
a statue? So, if we could go into this

88
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:18,860
question of what is beauty, I feel to really

89
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:25,360
go into it very deeply, one must know what

90
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:32,360
is suffering, or understand what is suffering,

91
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:39,360
because without passion you can't have beauty.

92
00:07:40,360 --> 00:07:47,860
Passion in the sense, not lust, not the

93
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:55,360
passion that comes when there is immense suffering,

94
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:59,860
and the remaining with that suffering, not

95
00:07:59,860 --> 00:08:04,360
escaping from it, brings this passion. Perhaps?

96
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:12,860
And passion means the abandonment, the complete abandonment

97
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:21,360
of the me, of the self, the ego,

98
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:27,360
and therefore a great austerity, not the austerity

99
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:33,360
of – as the word means – ash,

100
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:41,360
severe, dry, which the religious people have

101
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:49,360
made into, but rather the austerity of great

102
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:50,360
beauty.

103
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:56,360
A. Yes, I am following you, I really am.

104
00:08:56,360 --> 00:09:04,860
A great sense of dignity, beauty, that

105
00:09:04,860 --> 00:09:13,360
is essentially austere. And to be austere, not

106
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:19,360
verbally or ideologically, but being austere,

107
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:25,360
means total abandonment, letting go of the

108
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:32,860
me. And one cannot let that thing take

109
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:40,360
place if one hasn't deeply understood what suffering

110
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:44,860
is. Because passion comes from the word sorrow.

111
00:09:44,860 --> 00:09:49,360
I don't know if you have looked into that

112
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:53,360
word. The root meaning of the

113
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:57,360
word passion is sorrow, from suffering.

114
00:09:57,360 --> 00:09:59,360
A. To feel.

115
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:06,360
To feel. You see, sir, people have escaped

116
00:10:06,360 --> 00:10:13,360
from suffering. I think it is very deeply

117
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:16,360
related to beauty. Not that you must

118
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:19,360
suffer. I don't know if I am...

119
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:21,360
A. Not that you must suffer, but...

120
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:27,860
No, we must go a little more slowly. I

121
00:10:27,860 --> 00:10:34,360
am jumping too quickly. First of all, we assume

122
00:10:34,360 --> 00:10:41,860
we know what beauty is. We see Picasso

123
00:10:41,860 --> 00:10:49,360
or Rembrandt or Michelangelo, and we think, how

124
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:54,860
marvellous that is. We think we know. We

125
00:10:54,860 --> 00:11:00,360
have read it in books, experts have written about

126
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:07,360
it, and so on. One reads and says, yes,

127
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:14,360
we absorb it through others. But if one was

128
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:19,360
really enquiring into what is beauty, there must

129
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:24,360
be a great sense of humility. I don't

130
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:27,860
know what beauty is, actually. I can imagine

131
00:11:27,860 --> 00:11:31,360
what beauty is. I have learnt what beauty

132
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:36,360
is. I have been taught in schools, in

133
00:11:36,360 --> 00:11:41,360
colleges, reading books and going on tours, guided tours

134
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:46,360
and all the rest, visiting thousand museums. But

135
00:11:46,360 --> 00:11:51,360
actually, to find out the depth of beauty,

136
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:55,360
the depth of colour, the depth of feeling,

137
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:59,360
the mind must start with great sense of humility.

138
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:07,360
I don't know. Really, one doesn't know

139
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:15,360
what meditation is. One thinks, one knows. When

140
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:19,360
we discuss meditation, we'll come... So, one must

141
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:23,360
start, I feel, if one is enquiring into

142
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:30,360
beauty with great sense of humility, not

143
00:12:30,360 --> 00:12:37,360
knowing. That very not knowing is beauty.

144
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:45,860
A. Yes, yes. I have been listening and I

145
00:12:45,860 --> 00:12:54,360
have been trying to open myself to this relation

146
00:12:54,360 --> 00:13:03,360
that you are making between beauty and passion. And...

147
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:08,860
K. You see, sir, let's start. Man

148
00:13:08,860 --> 00:13:14,360
suffers, not only personally, but there is immense

149
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:21,860
suffering of man. It is a thing that

150
00:13:21,860 --> 00:13:29,360
is pervading the universe. Man has suffered physically,

151
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:33,360
psychologically, spiritually, in every way for

152
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:37,360
centuries upon centuries. The mother cries

153
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:40,360
because her son is killed. The mother cries

154
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:43,360
or the wife cries because her husband is being

155
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:50,860
mutilated in a war. Or there is tremendous

156
00:13:50,860 --> 00:13:58,360
suffering in the world. I don't think people

157
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:05,360
are aware or even feel this immense sorrow

158
00:14:05,360 --> 00:14:12,360
that is in the world. They are so concerned

159
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:19,360
with their own personal sorrow, they overlook the

160
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:26,360
sorrow that a poor man in a little village

161
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:32,360
in India or in China or in the Eastern

162
00:14:32,360 --> 00:14:38,360
world, will never possibly have a full meal, clean

163
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:44,360
clothes, comfortable bed. And there is the

164
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:50,360
sorrow of thousands of people being killed

165
00:14:50,360 --> 00:14:56,360
in war or in the totalitarian world,

166
00:14:56,360 --> 00:15:02,360
millions being executed for ideologies, tyranny, the

167
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:06,860
terror of all that. So there is all this

168
00:15:06,860 --> 00:15:11,360
sorrow in the world. And there is also the personal

169
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:17,860
sorrow. And without really understanding it

170
00:15:17,860 --> 00:15:24,360
very, very, very deeply and resolving it,

171
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:32,360
passion won't come out of sorrow. And without

172
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:40,360
passion, how can you see beauty? You can intellectually

173
00:15:40,360 --> 00:15:47,860
appreciate a painting or a poem or a statue,

174
00:15:47,860 --> 00:15:55,360
but you need this great sense of inward bursting

175
00:15:55,360 --> 00:16:02,360
of passion, exploding of passion. That creates

176
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:09,360
in itself the sensitivity that can say beauty.

177
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:14,860
So it is, I think, rather important to

178
00:16:14,860 --> 00:16:20,360
understand sorrow. I think it is related beauty, passion,

179
00:16:20,360 --> 00:16:22,360
sorrow.

180
00:16:22,360 --> 00:16:29,360
A. I am interested in the order of those

181
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:36,360
words, beauty, passion, sorrow. If one is, in relation

182
00:16:36,360 --> 00:16:40,860
to the transformation we have been speaking about, to

183
00:16:40,860 --> 00:16:45,360
come to beauty, I take it as a passage

184
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:50,360
from sorrow to passion to beauty.

185
00:16:50,360 --> 00:16:55,360
Yes, please do go on. I understand.

186
00:16:55,360 --> 00:17:02,359
K. You see, in the Christian world, if I

187
00:17:02,359 --> 00:17:09,359
am not mistaken, sorrow is delegated to a person.

188
00:17:09,359 --> 00:17:12,859
We either take that person or we somehow escape

189
00:17:12,859 --> 00:17:16,359
from sorrow, or at least we hope to escape

190
00:17:16,359 --> 00:17:22,859
from sorrow. And in the Eastern world,

191
00:17:22,859 --> 00:17:29,360
sorrow is rationalised through the statement of karma.

192
00:17:29,360 --> 00:17:32,360
You know the word karma means to do. And

193
00:17:32,360 --> 00:17:35,360
they believe in karma. That is what you have done

194
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:38,360
in your past life, you pay for it in the present

195
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:41,360
or reward it in the present, and so on and so on.

196
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:47,860
So there are these two categories of escapes.

197
00:17:47,860 --> 00:17:54,360
And there are a thousand escapes – whiskey,

198
00:17:54,360 --> 00:18:01,360
sex, going off to attend the mass and so on

199
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:08,360
and so on. Man has never stayed with a thing.

200
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:12,860
He has always either sought comfort in a

201
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:17,360
belief, in an action, in identification with something

202
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:21,860
greater than himself and so on and so on, but

203
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:26,360
he has never said, look, I must see what this is.

204
00:18:26,360 --> 00:18:31,360
I must penetrate it and not delegate it to somebody

205
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:36,360
else. I must go into it, I must face it,

206
00:18:36,360 --> 00:18:43,360
I must look at it, I must know what it

207
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:50,360
is. So when the mind doesn't escape from this sorrow,

208
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:55,360
either personal or the sorrow of man,

209
00:18:55,360 --> 00:19:00,360
if you don't escape, if you don't rationalise,

210
00:19:00,360 --> 00:19:06,360
if you don't try to go beyond it, if you

211
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:12,360
are not frightened of it, then you remain with it.

212
00:19:12,360 --> 00:19:18,860
Because any movement from what is, any movement

213
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:25,360
away from what is, is a dissipation of energy.

214
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:29,360
It prevents you actually understanding what

215
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:33,360
is. The what is is sorrow.

216
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:38,860
And we have means and

217
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:44,360
ways and cunning, development of escapes.

218
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:48,860
Now, if there is no escape

219
00:19:48,860 --> 00:19:53,360
whatsoever, then you remain with it.

220
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:59,360
I don't know if you have ever done it.

221
00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:05,360
Because in every one's life there is an

222
00:20:05,360 --> 00:20:11,360
incident that brings you tremendous sorrow, a happening.

223
00:20:11,360 --> 00:20:18,860
It might be an incident, a word, an accident,

224
00:20:18,860 --> 00:20:26,360
a shattering sense of absolute loneliness, and so on.

225
00:20:26,360 --> 00:20:31,360
These things happen, and with that

226
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:36,360
comes this sense of utter sorrow.

227
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:42,860
Now, when the mind can remain with that, not

228
00:20:42,860 --> 00:20:49,360
move away from it, out of that comes passion.

229
00:20:49,360 --> 00:20:53,860
Not the cultivated passion, not

230
00:20:53,860 --> 00:20:58,360
the artificial trying to be passionate,

231
00:20:58,360 --> 00:21:05,860
but the movement of passion is

232
00:21:05,860 --> 00:21:13,360
born out of this non-withdrawal from sorrow.

233
00:21:13,360 --> 00:21:21,360
It is the total, completely remaining with that.

234
00:21:21,360 --> 00:21:26,360
A. I am thinking that we also say, when

235
00:21:26,360 --> 00:21:31,360
we speak of someone in sorrow, that they are disconsolate.

236
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:33,360
K. Yes, disconsolate.

237
00:21:33,360 --> 00:21:38,860
A. Disconsolate. And immediately we think that the antidote

238
00:21:38,860 --> 00:21:44,360
to that is to get rid of the disc,

239
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:48,360
not to stay with the disc.

240
00:21:48,360 --> 00:21:51,860
And in an earlier conversation we spoke

241
00:21:51,860 --> 00:21:55,360
about two things related to each other

242
00:21:55,360 --> 00:21:58,360
in terms of opposite sides of the same coin.

243
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:03,860
And while you've been speaking, I've been seeing the

244
00:22:03,860 --> 00:22:09,360
interrelation in a polar sense between action and passion.

245
00:22:09,360 --> 00:22:15,360
Action being able to undergo, able to be

246
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:21,360
changed, whereas action is doing to effect change.

247
00:22:21,360 --> 00:22:24,860
And this would be the movement from

248
00:22:24,860 --> 00:22:28,360
sorrow to passion at the precise point,

249
00:22:28,360 --> 00:22:31,860
if I've understood you correctly, where I

250
00:22:31,860 --> 00:22:35,360
become able to undergo what is there.

251
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:41,360
So, when there is no escape, when there is

252
00:22:41,360 --> 00:22:47,360
no desire to seek comfort away from what is,

253
00:22:47,360 --> 00:22:54,360
then out of that absolute inescapable

254
00:22:54,360 --> 00:23:01,360
reality comes this flame of passion.

255
00:23:01,360 --> 00:23:04,360
And without that there is no beauty.

256
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:09,360
You may write endless volumes about

257
00:23:09,360 --> 00:23:14,360
beauty, or be a marvellous painter,

258
00:23:14,360 --> 00:23:19,360
but without that inward quality of passion,

259
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:24,360
which is the outcome of great understanding of sorrow,

260
00:23:24,360 --> 00:23:30,360
I don't see how beauty can exist.

261
00:23:30,360 --> 00:23:39,360
Also, one observes, man has lost touch with nature.

262
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:41,360
A. Oh, yes.

263
00:23:41,360 --> 00:23:45,360
K. Completely, especially in big towns.

264
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:50,360
And even in small towns or

265
00:23:50,360 --> 00:23:55,360
hamlets, man is always outwardly going, outward,

266
00:23:55,360 --> 00:23:59,360
pursued by his own thoughts.

267
00:23:59,360 --> 00:24:02,860
And so he has more

268
00:24:02,860 --> 00:24:06,360
or less lost touch with nature.

269
00:24:06,360 --> 00:24:08,360
Nature means nothing to him.

270
00:24:08,360 --> 00:24:10,360
Yes, it's very nice, very beautiful.

271
00:24:10,360 --> 00:24:14,360
Once I was standing with a few

272
00:24:14,360 --> 00:24:18,360
friends and my brother many years ago

273
00:24:18,360 --> 00:24:24,360
over Grand Canyon, looking at the morns, incredible,

274
00:24:24,360 --> 00:24:27,360
the colours, the depth and the shadows,

275
00:24:27,360 --> 00:24:32,360
and a group of people came and one lady says,

276
00:24:32,360 --> 00:24:35,360
yes, isn't this marvellous?

277
00:24:35,360 --> 00:24:36,360
And the next day she

278
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:37,360
says, let's go and have tea.

279
00:24:37,360 --> 00:24:39,360
And off they trotted.

280
00:24:39,360 --> 00:24:41,360
That took her.

281
00:24:41,360 --> 00:24:45,360
That is what is happening in the world.

282
00:24:45,360 --> 00:24:50,360
We have lost completely touch with nature.

283
00:24:50,360 --> 00:24:53,360
We don't know what it means.

284
00:24:53,360 --> 00:24:58,360
And also we kill. You follow?

285
00:24:58,360 --> 00:25:01,360
We kill for food, we kill for amusement,

286
00:25:01,360 --> 00:25:05,360
we kill what is called for sport.

287
00:25:07,360 --> 00:25:09,360
I won't go into all that.

288
00:25:09,360 --> 00:25:17,360
So there is this lack of intimate relationship with nature.

289
00:25:17,360 --> 00:25:20,360
I remember a shock, a profound shock

290
00:25:20,360 --> 00:25:23,360
that I had in my college days.

291
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:27,360
I was standing on the steps of the administration building

292
00:25:27,360 --> 00:25:31,360
and watching a very, very beautiful sunset.

293
00:25:31,360 --> 00:25:35,360
And one of my college quents

294
00:25:35,360 --> 00:25:39,360
asked me what I was doing.

295
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:43,360
And I said, well, I'm not doing anything,

296
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:48,360
I'm looking at the sunset.

297
00:25:48,360 --> 00:25:51,360
And you know what he said to me?

298
00:25:51,360 --> 00:25:54,360
This so shocked me that it's one of those things

299
00:25:54,360 --> 00:25:55,360
that you never forget.

300
00:25:55,360 --> 00:25:56,860
He just said, well, there is

301
00:25:56,860 --> 00:25:58,360
nothing to prevent it, is there?

302
00:25:58,360 --> 00:25:59,360
Nothing?

303
00:25:59,360 --> 00:26:01,360
Nothing to prevent it, is there?

304
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:03,360
Right, right.

305
00:26:03,360 --> 00:26:06,360
Yes, I know, I follow you.

306
00:26:06,360 --> 00:26:09,860
So, sir, you see, we

307
00:26:09,860 --> 00:26:13,360
are becoming more and more artificial,

308
00:26:13,360 --> 00:26:17,360
more and more superficial,

309
00:26:17,360 --> 00:26:22,360
more and more verbal,

310
00:26:26,360 --> 00:26:30,360
a linear direction,

311
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:35,360
neither vertical, not vertical at all, but linear.

312
00:26:35,360 --> 00:26:43,360
And so naturally, artificial things become more important,

313
00:26:43,360 --> 00:26:46,360
theatres, cinemas,

314
00:26:46,360 --> 00:26:51,360
you know, the whole business of the modern world.

315
00:26:52,360 --> 00:27:02,360
And very few have this sense of beauty in themselves,

316
00:27:02,360 --> 00:27:04,360
beauty in conduct.

317
00:27:04,360 --> 00:27:05,360
You understand, sir?

318
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:06,360
A. Oh, yes.

319
00:27:06,360 --> 00:27:09,360
K. Beauty in behaviour,

320
00:27:09,360 --> 00:27:15,360
beauty in the usage of their language, the voice,

321
00:27:15,360 --> 00:27:22,360
the manner of walking, the sense of humility,

322
00:27:22,360 --> 00:27:27,360
with that humility everything becomes so gentle, quiet,

323
00:27:27,360 --> 00:27:29,360
full of beauty.

324
00:27:29,360 --> 00:27:32,360
We have none of that.

325
00:27:32,360 --> 00:27:36,360
And yet we go to museums, you know,

326
00:27:36,360 --> 00:27:41,360
we are educated with museums, with pictures,

327
00:27:41,360 --> 00:27:46,360
and we have lost the delicacy, the sensitivity

328
00:27:46,360 --> 00:27:49,360
of the mind, the heart, the body.

329
00:27:49,360 --> 00:27:51,360
And so when we have lost

330
00:27:51,360 --> 00:27:53,360
the sensitivity, how can we be,

331
00:27:53,360 --> 00:27:55,360
how can we know what beauty is?

332
00:27:55,360 --> 00:27:57,360
And when we haven't got sensitivity,

333
00:27:57,360 --> 00:28:00,360
we go off to some

334
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:03,360
place to learn to be sensitive.

335
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:04,360
You know that.

336
00:28:04,360 --> 00:28:05,360
A. No, I do.

337
00:28:05,360 --> 00:28:09,360
K. Go to a college or some ashrama

338
00:28:09,360 --> 00:28:11,360
or some rotten hole,

339
00:28:11,360 --> 00:28:15,360
and there I am going to learn to be sensitive.

340
00:28:15,360 --> 00:28:18,360
Sensitive through touch, through...

341
00:28:18,360 --> 00:28:20,360
A. Yes.

342
00:28:20,360 --> 00:28:24,360
K. That becomes disgusting.

343
00:28:29,360 --> 00:28:31,860
Now how can we, as

344
00:28:31,860 --> 00:28:34,360
you are a professor and teacher,

345
00:28:34,360 --> 00:28:39,360
how can you, sir, educate

346
00:28:39,360 --> 00:28:41,360
– it becomes really very important –

347
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:48,360
the students to have this quality?

348
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:52,360
Therefore one asks, what is it we are educated for?

349
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:57,360
What are we being educated for?

350
00:28:57,360 --> 00:29:02,360
Everybody is being educated.

351
00:29:02,360 --> 00:29:05,360
Ninety percent of the people probably in America

352
00:29:05,360 --> 00:29:07,360
are being educated,

353
00:29:07,360 --> 00:29:08,360
know what to read and write

354
00:29:08,360 --> 00:29:09,360
and all the rest of it.

355
00:29:09,360 --> 00:29:11,360
What for?

356
00:29:11,360 --> 00:29:12,860
A. And yet it is a

357
00:29:12,860 --> 00:29:14,360
fact, at least in my experience

358
00:29:14,360 --> 00:29:18,360
of teaching class after class, year after year,

359
00:29:18,360 --> 00:29:23,360
that with all this proliferation of publishing

360
00:29:24,360 --> 00:29:28,360
and so-called educational techniques,

361
00:29:28,360 --> 00:29:34,360
students are without as much care

362
00:29:34,360 --> 00:29:38,360
to the written word and the spoken word

363
00:29:38,360 --> 00:29:40,860
as was the case that

364
00:29:40,860 --> 00:29:43,360
I can distinctly remember years ago.

365
00:29:43,360 --> 00:29:46,360
Now perhaps other teachers have had a different experience,

366
00:29:46,360 --> 00:29:49,360
but I have watched this in my classes.

367
00:29:49,360 --> 00:29:51,360
And the usual answer that I get

368
00:29:51,360 --> 00:29:53,360
when I speak to my colleagues about this is,

369
00:29:53,360 --> 00:29:55,360
well, the problem is in the high schools.

370
00:29:55,360 --> 00:29:57,360
And then you talk to a poor high school.

371
00:29:57,360 --> 00:29:59,360
K. And then it is put out into the school.

372
00:29:59,360 --> 00:30:00,860
A. And then he puts

373
00:30:00,860 --> 00:30:02,360
it on the poor grade school.

374
00:30:02,360 --> 00:30:05,360
So we have poor grade school, poor high school,

375
00:30:05,360 --> 00:30:09,360
poor college, poor university,

376
00:30:09,360 --> 00:30:11,360
because we are always picking up where we left off,

377
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:13,360
which is a little lower next

378
00:30:13,360 --> 00:30:15,360
year from where it was before.

379
00:30:15,360 --> 00:30:17,360
K. Sir, that is why

380
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:19,360
I have talked in various universities

381
00:30:19,360 --> 00:30:20,360
and so on and so on.

382
00:30:20,360 --> 00:30:25,360
I have always felt, what are we being educated for?

383
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:31,360
To just become glorified clerks?

384
00:30:31,360 --> 00:30:34,360
A. Well, that is what it turns out to be.

385
00:30:34,360 --> 00:30:36,360
K. Of course it is.

386
00:30:36,360 --> 00:30:40,360
Glorified businessmen, or God knows what else.

387
00:30:40,360 --> 00:30:42,360
What for?

388
00:30:42,360 --> 00:30:45,360
I mean, if I have a son,

389
00:30:45,360 --> 00:30:49,360
that would be a tremendous problem for me.

390
00:30:49,360 --> 00:30:50,360
Fortunately, I haven't got sick,

391
00:30:50,360 --> 00:30:54,360
but it would be a burning question to me,

392
00:30:54,360 --> 00:30:56,860
what am I to do

393
00:30:56,860 --> 00:30:59,360
with the children that I have?

394
00:30:59,360 --> 00:31:03,360
To centralise all these schools

395
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:07,360
where they are taught nothing but just

396
00:31:07,360 --> 00:31:09,360
how to read and write

397
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:11,360
a book and how to memorise

398
00:31:11,360 --> 00:31:15,360
and forget the whole field of life.

399
00:31:16,360 --> 00:31:20,360
They are taught about sex and reproduction,

400
00:31:20,360 --> 00:31:25,360
all that kind of stuff, but what?

401
00:31:25,360 --> 00:31:27,860
So I feel, sir, I mean,

402
00:31:27,860 --> 00:31:30,360
to me this is a tremendous,

403
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:31,360
the important question,

404
00:31:31,360 --> 00:31:34,360
is what is the point of education?

405
00:31:34,360 --> 00:31:37,360
To me this is a tremendous, the important question,

406
00:31:37,360 --> 00:31:40,360
because I am concerned with several schools in India,

407
00:31:40,360 --> 00:31:42,360
and in England there is one,

408
00:31:42,360 --> 00:31:45,360
we are going to form one here in California.

409
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:47,360
It is a burning question,

410
00:31:47,360 --> 00:31:52,360
what is it that we are doing with our children?

411
00:31:53,360 --> 00:31:55,360
Making them into another robot,

412
00:31:55,360 --> 00:32:00,360
or into other clever cunning clerks,

413
00:32:00,360 --> 00:32:03,360
great scientists who will invent this or that

414
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:08,360
and then be ordinary cheap little human beings?

415
00:32:09,360 --> 00:32:11,360
Sod the minds? You follow, sir?

416
00:32:11,360 --> 00:32:15,360
A. I am, I am, I am.

417
00:32:15,360 --> 00:32:20,360
K. So when you talk about beauty,

418
00:32:23,360 --> 00:32:29,360
can we, can a human being tell another,

419
00:32:29,360 --> 00:32:34,360
educate another to grow in beauty,

420
00:32:34,360 --> 00:32:37,360
grow in goodness,

421
00:32:37,360 --> 00:32:43,360
to flower in great affection, care?

422
00:32:43,360 --> 00:32:45,360
Because if we don't do that,

423
00:32:45,360 --> 00:32:50,360
we are destroying the earth as it is happening now,

424
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:52,360
polluting the air.

425
00:32:52,360 --> 00:32:58,360
We human beings are destroying everything we touch.

426
00:33:00,360 --> 00:33:09,360
So this becomes a very, very serious thing

427
00:33:09,360 --> 00:33:11,360
when we talk about beauty,

428
00:33:11,360 --> 00:33:15,360
when we talk about pleasure, fear,

429
00:33:15,360 --> 00:33:20,360
relationship, order and so on, all that,

430
00:33:20,360 --> 00:33:25,360
none of these things are being taught in any school.

431
00:33:26,360 --> 00:33:29,360
A. No, I brought that up in my class yesterday,

432
00:33:29,360 --> 00:33:34,360
and I asked them directly that very question,

433
00:33:34,360 --> 00:33:37,360
and they were very ready

434
00:33:37,360 --> 00:33:40,360
to agree that here we are,

435
00:33:40,360 --> 00:33:44,360
we are in an upper division course,

436
00:33:44,360 --> 00:33:48,360
and we have never heard about this.

437
00:33:48,360 --> 00:33:50,360
K. Tragic, you follow, sir?

438
00:33:50,360 --> 00:33:52,360
A. And furthermore, we don't know

439
00:33:52,360 --> 00:33:53,860
whether we are really hearing

440
00:33:53,860 --> 00:33:55,360
it for what it really is,

441
00:33:55,360 --> 00:33:57,360
because we haven't heard about it.

442
00:33:57,360 --> 00:33:59,360
We have got to go through that yet

443
00:33:59,360 --> 00:34:02,360
to find out whether we are really listening.

444
00:34:02,360 --> 00:34:03,860
K. And whether the teacher or

445
00:34:03,860 --> 00:34:05,360
the man who is a professor

446
00:34:05,360 --> 00:34:09,359
is honest enough to say, look, I don't know,

447
00:34:09,359 --> 00:34:13,359
I am going to learn about all this.

448
00:34:16,359 --> 00:34:20,359
So, sir, that is why Western civilisation

449
00:34:20,360 --> 00:34:21,860
– I am not condemning

450
00:34:21,860 --> 00:34:23,360
it, I am observing it –

451
00:34:23,360 --> 00:34:28,360
Western civilisation is mainly concerned

452
00:34:28,360 --> 00:34:33,360
with commercialism, consumerism

453
00:34:33,360 --> 00:34:39,360
and a society that is immoral.

454
00:34:42,360 --> 00:34:47,360
And when we talk about the transformation of man,

455
00:34:48,360 --> 00:34:49,860
not in the field of knowledge

456
00:34:49,860 --> 00:34:51,360
or in the field of time,

457
00:34:51,360 --> 00:34:55,360
but beyond that,

458
00:34:55,360 --> 00:34:59,360
who are interested in this?

459
00:34:59,360 --> 00:35:04,360
You follow, sir? Who really cares about it?

460
00:35:04,360 --> 00:35:07,360
Because the mother goes off to earn a job,

461
00:35:07,360 --> 00:35:09,360
earn a livelihood, the father goes off,

462
00:35:09,360 --> 00:35:14,360
and the child is just an incident.

463
00:35:14,360 --> 00:35:16,360
Now, as a matter of fact,

464
00:35:16,360 --> 00:35:18,360
I know this will probably appear

465
00:35:18,360 --> 00:35:22,360
like an astonishingly extravagant statement for me to make,

466
00:35:22,360 --> 00:35:26,360
but I think it is getting to the place now

467
00:35:26,360 --> 00:35:30,360
where if anyone raises this question

468
00:35:30,360 --> 00:35:33,360
at the level that you have been raising it,

469
00:35:33,360 --> 00:35:36,860
as a young person, say,

470
00:35:36,860 --> 00:35:40,360
growing up in his adolescent years,

471
00:35:40,360 --> 00:35:44,360
as I say, and he won't let it go,

472
00:35:44,360 --> 00:35:48,360
he hangs in there with it, as we say,

473
00:35:48,360 --> 00:35:52,360
the question is seriously raised whether he is normal.

474
00:35:52,360 --> 00:35:54,360
Yes, quite, quite, quite.

475
00:35:54,360 --> 00:35:57,360
And it makes one think of Socrates,

476
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:01,360
who was very clear that he knew only one thing

477
00:36:01,360 --> 00:36:03,360
that he didn't know,

478
00:36:03,360 --> 00:36:05,360
and he didn't have to say that very often,

479
00:36:05,360 --> 00:36:07,860
but he said it even the

480
00:36:07,860 --> 00:36:10,360
few times enough to get him killed,

481
00:36:10,360 --> 00:36:11,860
but at least they took

482
00:36:11,860 --> 00:36:13,360
him seriously enough to kill him.

483
00:36:13,360 --> 00:36:15,860
Today I think he would be

484
00:36:15,860 --> 00:36:18,360
put in some institution for study.

485
00:36:18,360 --> 00:36:20,360
The whole thing would have to be checked out.

486
00:36:20,360 --> 00:36:22,360
That's what is happening in Russia.

487
00:36:22,360 --> 00:36:24,360
They send him off to an asylum,

488
00:36:24,360 --> 00:36:29,360
mental hospital, and destroy him.

489
00:36:30,360 --> 00:36:37,360
Sir, here we neglect everything

490
00:36:37,360 --> 00:36:41,360
for some superficial gain.

491
00:36:41,360 --> 00:36:43,360
Money.

492
00:36:43,360 --> 00:36:49,360
Money means power, position, authority, everything, money.

493
00:36:51,360 --> 00:36:52,860
It goes back to the

494
00:36:52,860 --> 00:36:54,360
success thing that you mentioned before.

495
00:36:54,360 --> 00:36:58,360
Always later, always later,

496
00:36:58,360 --> 00:37:02,360
on the horizontal axis.

497
00:37:02,360 --> 00:37:04,360
But when you... excuse me.

498
00:37:04,360 --> 00:37:07,360
I did want to share with you,

499
00:37:07,360 --> 00:37:09,360
as you were speaking about nature,

500
00:37:09,360 --> 00:37:15,360
something that has a sort of wry humour about it

501
00:37:15,360 --> 00:37:19,360
in terms of the history of scholarship.

502
00:37:19,360 --> 00:37:23,360
I thought of those marvellous Vedic hymns to dawn,

503
00:37:23,360 --> 00:37:30,360
which, Dakshinara, that the way dawn comes,

504
00:37:30,360 --> 00:37:32,360
rosy-fingered,

505
00:37:32,360 --> 00:37:37,360
and scholars have expressed surprise

506
00:37:37,360 --> 00:37:40,360
that the number of hymns

507
00:37:40,360 --> 00:37:43,360
to her are, by comparison, few,

508
00:37:43,360 --> 00:37:47,360
compared with some other gods.

509
00:37:48,360 --> 00:37:53,360
But the attention is drawn in the study,

510
00:37:53,360 --> 00:37:56,360
not to the quality of the hymn,

511
00:37:56,360 --> 00:38:00,360
as revealing how it is that

512
00:38:00,360 --> 00:38:05,360
there are such consummately beautiful cadences

513
00:38:08,360 --> 00:38:11,360
associated with her,

514
00:38:11,360 --> 00:38:13,360
for which you'd only need one, wouldn't you?

515
00:38:13,360 --> 00:38:15,360
You wouldn't need twenty-five.

516
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:19,360
The important thing is, isn't it remarkable

517
00:38:19,360 --> 00:38:22,360
that we have so few hymns,

518
00:38:22,360 --> 00:38:26,360
and yet they're so wonderfully beautiful?

519
00:38:26,360 --> 00:38:30,360
What has the number to do with it at all?

520
00:38:30,360 --> 00:38:32,360
It is the thing that I

521
00:38:32,360 --> 00:38:34,360
could never get answered for myself

522
00:38:34,360 --> 00:38:37,360
in terms of the environment in which I studied

523
00:38:37,360 --> 00:38:39,360
Sanskrit and the Veda.

524
00:38:39,360 --> 00:38:42,360
The important thing is to find out which god,

525
00:38:42,360 --> 00:38:45,360
which is Indra, is in the Rig Veda,

526
00:38:45,360 --> 00:38:47,360
mentioned most often.

527
00:38:47,360 --> 00:38:50,360
Now, of course, I'm not trying to suggest

528
00:38:50,360 --> 00:38:53,360
that quantity should be overlooked by no means,

529
00:38:53,360 --> 00:38:57,360
but if the question had been approached

530
00:38:57,360 --> 00:39:03,360
the way you have been inquiring into it,

531
00:39:03,360 --> 00:39:06,360
deeper, deeper, deeper,

532
00:39:06,360 --> 00:39:09,360
then I think scholarship would have had

533
00:39:09,360 --> 00:39:11,360
a very, very different career.

534
00:39:11,360 --> 00:39:14,360
We should have been taught how to sit

535
00:39:14,360 --> 00:39:17,360
and let that hymn disclose itself,

536
00:39:17,360 --> 00:39:19,360
and stop measuring it.

537
00:39:19,360 --> 00:39:20,360
Yes, sir.

538
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:22,360
Yes, please, please do go on.

539
00:39:22,360 --> 00:39:25,360
That's what I was going to say.

540
00:39:26,360 --> 00:39:32,360
You see, when discussing beauty and passion and sorrow,

541
00:39:32,360 --> 00:39:35,360
we ought to go into the question also,

542
00:39:35,360 --> 00:39:37,360
what is action?

543
00:39:37,360 --> 00:39:38,360
Yes.

544
00:39:38,360 --> 00:39:40,360
Because it is related to all that.

545
00:39:40,360 --> 00:39:42,360
Yes, of course.

546
00:39:42,360 --> 00:39:45,360
What is action?

547
00:39:45,360 --> 00:39:48,360
Because life is action,

548
00:39:48,360 --> 00:39:50,360
living is action,

549
00:39:50,360 --> 00:39:53,360
speaking is action, everything is action.

550
00:39:53,360 --> 00:39:56,360
Sitting here is an action,

551
00:39:56,360 --> 00:40:00,360
talking, a dialogue, discussing, going into things,

552
00:40:00,360 --> 00:40:06,360
is a series of actions, a movement in action.

553
00:40:07,360 --> 00:40:10,360
So, what is action?

554
00:40:11,360 --> 00:40:16,360
Action obviously means acting now,

555
00:40:16,360 --> 00:40:21,360
not having acted or will act.

556
00:40:21,360 --> 00:40:23,860
It is the active present

557
00:40:23,860 --> 00:40:26,360
of the word act, to act,

558
00:40:26,360 --> 00:40:32,360
which is acting all the time.

559
00:40:33,360 --> 00:40:35,360
Otherwise there is no...

560
00:40:35,360 --> 00:40:43,360
it is a movement in time and out of time.

561
00:40:43,360 --> 00:40:47,360
We will go into that a little bit later.

562
00:40:47,360 --> 00:40:53,360
Now, so what is action that does not bring sorrow?

563
00:40:53,360 --> 00:40:56,360
You follow?

564
00:40:57,360 --> 00:41:00,360
One has to put that question,

565
00:41:00,360 --> 00:41:03,360
because every action, as we do now,

566
00:41:03,360 --> 00:41:08,360
is either regret, contradiction,

567
00:41:08,360 --> 00:41:15,360
a sense of meaningless movement,

568
00:41:15,360 --> 00:41:21,360
a repression, conformity and so on.

569
00:41:21,360 --> 00:41:25,360
So that is action for most people.

570
00:41:25,360 --> 00:41:28,360
The routine, the repetition,

571
00:41:28,360 --> 00:41:31,360
the remembrance of things past

572
00:41:31,360 --> 00:41:36,360
and act according to that remembrance.

573
00:41:37,360 --> 00:41:45,360
So, unless one understands very deeply what is action,

574
00:41:46,360 --> 00:41:52,360
one will not be able to understand what is sorrow.

575
00:41:52,360 --> 00:41:57,360
So, action, sorrow, passion and beauty.

576
00:41:58,360 --> 00:42:01,360
They are all together, not divorced,

577
00:42:01,360 --> 00:42:03,360
not something separate,

578
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:06,360
beauty is at the end, action is at the beginning.

579
00:42:06,360 --> 00:42:08,360
It isn't like that at

580
00:42:08,360 --> 00:42:10,360
all, it is all one thing.

581
00:42:10,360 --> 00:42:16,360
But to look at it, what is action?

582
00:42:17,360 --> 00:42:20,360
As far as one knows now,

583
00:42:20,360 --> 00:42:25,360
action is according to a formula,

584
00:42:26,360 --> 00:42:33,360
according to a concept, or according to an ideology,

585
00:42:33,360 --> 00:42:37,360
the communist ideology, the capitalist ideology,

586
00:42:37,360 --> 00:42:39,360
or socialist ideology,

587
00:42:39,360 --> 00:42:43,360
or the ideology of a Christian, Jesus, Christ,

588
00:42:43,360 --> 00:42:46,360
or the Hindu with his ideologies.

589
00:42:46,360 --> 00:42:53,360
So, action is the approximation

590
00:42:55,360 --> 00:43:00,360
of an idea.

591
00:43:00,360 --> 00:43:06,360
I act according to my concept.

592
00:43:06,360 --> 00:43:10,360
That concept is traditional,

593
00:43:10,360 --> 00:43:14,360
or put together by me,

594
00:43:14,360 --> 00:43:18,360
or put together by an expert.

595
00:43:18,360 --> 00:43:23,360
Lenin, Marx have formulated,

596
00:43:23,360 --> 00:43:30,360
and they conform according to what they think Lenin, Marx,

597
00:43:30,360 --> 00:43:36,360
and action is according to a pattern.

598
00:43:36,360 --> 00:43:37,360
You follow?

599
00:43:37,360 --> 00:43:38,360
A. Yes, I do.

600
00:43:38,360 --> 00:43:40,360
What is occurring to me is that

601
00:43:40,360 --> 00:43:45,360
under the tyranny of that one is literally driven.

602
00:43:45,360 --> 00:43:46,360
K. Absolutely.

603
00:43:46,360 --> 00:43:50,360
Driven, conditioned, brutalised.

604
00:43:50,360 --> 00:43:54,360
You don't care for anything except for ideas,

605
00:43:54,360 --> 00:43:58,360
and carrying out ideas.

606
00:43:58,360 --> 00:44:02,360
See what is happening in China, in Russia.

607
00:44:02,360 --> 00:44:05,360
A. Yes, I do.

608
00:44:05,360 --> 00:44:07,860
K. And here too the

609
00:44:07,860 --> 00:44:10,360
same thing in a modified form.

610
00:44:10,360 --> 00:44:15,360
So, action as we know it now

611
00:44:15,360 --> 00:44:20,360
is conformity to a pattern,

612
00:44:20,360 --> 00:44:25,360
either in the future or in the past,

613
00:44:25,360 --> 00:44:30,360
an idea which I carry out,

614
00:44:30,360 --> 00:44:37,360
a resolution or a decision which I fulfil in acting.

615
00:44:37,360 --> 00:44:42,360
The past is acting.

616
00:44:42,360 --> 00:44:46,360
So it is not action.

617
00:44:46,360 --> 00:44:47,360
I don't know if you understand.

618
00:44:47,360 --> 00:44:57,360
A. Yes, I am aware of the fact that

619
00:44:57,360 --> 00:45:01,360
we suffer a radical conviction

620
00:45:01,360 --> 00:45:06,360
that if we don't generate a pattern,

621
00:45:06,360 --> 00:45:08,360
there will be no order.

622
00:45:08,360 --> 00:45:10,360
K. You follow what is happening, sir?

623
00:45:10,360 --> 00:45:13,360
Order is in terms of a pattern.

624
00:45:13,360 --> 00:45:18,360
A. Yes, preconceived. Yes.

625
00:45:18,360 --> 00:45:23,360
K. Therefore it is disorder

626
00:45:23,360 --> 00:45:29,360
against which an intelligent mind fights,

627
00:45:29,360 --> 00:45:33,360
fights in the sense, revolves.

628
00:45:33,360 --> 00:45:36,360
So that is why it is very important

629
00:45:36,360 --> 00:45:39,360
if we are to understand what beauty is,

630
00:45:39,360 --> 00:45:43,360
if we are to understand what action is.

631
00:45:43,360 --> 00:45:54,360
Can there be action without the idea?

632
00:45:54,360 --> 00:46:00,360
Idea means, you must know from Greek,

633
00:46:00,360 --> 00:46:04,360
means to see, see what we have done, sir.

634
00:46:04,360 --> 00:46:10,360
The word is to see.

635
00:46:10,360 --> 00:46:15,360
That is, seeing and the doing,

636
00:46:15,360 --> 00:46:20,360
not the seeing, draw a conclusion from that

637
00:46:20,360 --> 00:46:23,360
and then act according to that conclusion.

638
00:46:23,360 --> 00:46:26,360
You see?

639
00:46:26,360 --> 00:46:28,360
A. Oh yes, yes.

640
00:46:28,360 --> 00:46:34,360
K. Perceiving from that perception,

641
00:46:34,360 --> 00:46:42,360
draw a belief, an idea, a formula,

642
00:46:42,360 --> 00:46:47,360
and act according to that belief, idea, formula.

643
00:46:47,360 --> 00:46:52,360
So we are removed from perception.

644
00:46:52,360 --> 00:46:56,360
We are acting only according to a formula,

645
00:46:56,360 --> 00:47:00,360
therefore mechanical.

646
00:47:00,360 --> 00:47:06,360
You see, sir, how our minds have become mechanical.

647
00:47:06,360 --> 00:47:12,360
A. Necessarily so. K. Obviously.

648
00:47:12,360 --> 00:47:19,360
A. I just thought about Greek sculpture

649
00:47:19,360 --> 00:47:25,360
and its different character from Roman sculpture.

650
00:47:25,360 --> 00:47:32,360
The finest of ancient Greek sculpture

651
00:47:32,360 --> 00:47:37,360
is extremely contemplative.

652
00:47:37,360 --> 00:47:40,360
It has sometimes been remarked

653
00:47:40,360 --> 00:47:45,360
that the Romans had a genius for portraiture in stone.

654
00:47:45,360 --> 00:47:47,360
K. Law and order and all that.

655
00:47:47,360 --> 00:47:50,360
A. Yes, and of course one would see

656
00:47:50,360 --> 00:47:55,360
their remarkable attention to personality.

657
00:47:55,360 --> 00:47:56,360
But what occurred to me

658
00:47:56,360 --> 00:47:57,360
while I was listening to this,

659
00:47:57,360 --> 00:48:01,360
something that had never occurred to me before,

660
00:48:01,360 --> 00:48:10,360
that the Greek statue with which one sometimes asks himself,

661
00:48:10,360 --> 00:48:16,360
well, the face doesn't disclose a personality.

662
00:48:16,360 --> 00:48:24,360
Perhaps the quiet eye recognized

663
00:48:24,360 --> 00:48:28,360
that you don't put onto the stone

664
00:48:28,360 --> 00:48:40,360
something that must come out of the act itself.

665
00:48:40,360 --> 00:48:42,360
K. Quite, quite.

666
00:48:42,360 --> 00:48:48,360
A. Because you're doing something

667
00:48:48,360 --> 00:48:53,360
that you must wait to come to pass.

668
00:48:53,360 --> 00:48:57,360
The Greeks were correct.

669
00:48:57,360 --> 00:49:01,360
It's an expression of that relation to form,

670
00:49:01,360 --> 00:49:05,360
which is an interior form.

671
00:49:05,360 --> 00:49:10,360
Marvellous grasp of that.

672
00:49:10,360 --> 00:49:16,360
It's a grasp that allows for splendour to break out

673
00:49:16,360 --> 00:49:21,360
rather than the notion that we must represent it.

674
00:49:21,360 --> 00:49:23,360
Yes, I am following you, aren't I?

675
00:49:23,360 --> 00:49:25,860
K. You see, sir, that's why

676
00:49:25,860 --> 00:49:28,360
one must ask this essential question,

677
00:49:28,360 --> 00:49:32,360
what is action?

678
00:49:32,360 --> 00:49:35,360
Is it a repetition?

679
00:49:35,360 --> 00:49:38,360
Is it imitation?

680
00:49:38,360 --> 00:49:43,360
Is it an adjustment between what is

681
00:49:43,360 --> 00:49:48,360
and what should be or what has been?

682
00:49:48,360 --> 00:49:58,360
Or is it a conformity to a pattern,

683
00:49:58,360 --> 00:50:01,360
or to a belief, or to a formula?

684
00:50:01,360 --> 00:50:09,360
If it is, then inevitably there must be conflict.

685
00:50:09,360 --> 00:50:14,360
Because idea, action, there is an interval,

686
00:50:14,360 --> 00:50:17,360
a lag of time between the two,

687
00:50:17,360 --> 00:50:21,360
and in that interval a great many things happen,

688
00:50:21,360 --> 00:50:25,360
a division in which other incidents take place,

689
00:50:25,360 --> 00:50:28,360
and therefore there must be inevitably conflict,

690
00:50:28,360 --> 00:50:32,360
because action is never complete.

691
00:50:32,360 --> 00:50:38,360
Action is never total, it is never ending.

692
00:50:38,360 --> 00:50:43,360
Action means ending.

693
00:50:43,360 --> 00:50:47,360
You know, you used the word Vedanta the other day.

694
00:50:47,360 --> 00:50:54,360
It means the ending of knowledge, I was told.

695
00:50:54,360 --> 00:51:00,360
Not the continuous of knowledge, but the ending.

696
00:51:00,360 --> 00:51:03,360
That is, the freedom, all that.

697
00:51:03,360 --> 00:51:09,360
Now, is there an action

698
00:51:09,360 --> 00:51:17,360
which is not tied to the past as time,

699
00:51:17,360 --> 00:51:19,360
or to the future,

700
00:51:19,360 --> 00:51:20,860
or to a formula, or to

701
00:51:20,860 --> 00:51:22,360
a belief, or to an idea,

702
00:51:22,360 --> 00:51:24,360
but action?

703
00:51:24,360 --> 00:51:28,360
The seeing is the doing.

704
00:51:28,360 --> 00:51:32,360
A. Yes.

705
00:51:32,360 --> 00:51:35,360
K. The seeing is the doing

706
00:51:35,360 --> 00:51:43,360
becomes an extraordinary movement in freedom.

707
00:51:43,360 --> 00:51:47,360
The other is not freedom.

708
00:51:47,360 --> 00:51:50,360
And therefore, sir, the communists say

709
00:51:50,360 --> 00:51:52,360
there is no such thing as freedom.

710
00:51:52,360 --> 00:51:54,360
That is a bourgeois idea.

711
00:51:54,360 --> 00:51:56,360
Of course it is, bourgeois idea,

712
00:51:56,360 --> 00:52:05,360
because they live in ideas, concepts, not in action.

713
00:52:05,360 --> 00:52:09,360
They live according to ideas

714
00:52:09,360 --> 00:52:12,360
and carry those ideas out in action,

715
00:52:12,360 --> 00:52:18,360
which is not action, the doing.

716
00:52:18,360 --> 00:52:19,360
I don't know...

717
00:52:19,360 --> 00:52:21,360
A. Oh, yes, yes, I was just thinking...

718
00:52:21,360 --> 00:52:22,360
K. And this is what

719
00:52:22,360 --> 00:52:23,360
we do in the Western world,

720
00:52:23,360 --> 00:52:25,360
in the Eastern world, and all over the world,

721
00:52:25,360 --> 00:52:31,360
is acting according to a formula, idea,

722
00:52:31,360 --> 00:52:37,360
belief, a concept, a conclusion, a decision.

723
00:52:37,360 --> 00:52:43,360
And never the seeing and the doing.

724
00:52:43,360 --> 00:52:46,360
A. I was thinking about the cat,

725
00:52:46,360 --> 00:52:51,360
the marvellous animal, the cat.

726
00:52:51,360 --> 00:52:58,360
Its face is almost all eye.

727
00:52:58,360 --> 00:53:01,360
I don't mean that by

728
00:53:01,360 --> 00:53:04,360
measure, with calipers, of course not.

729
00:53:04,360 --> 00:53:06,360
And we don't train cats

730
00:53:06,360 --> 00:53:08,360
like we try to train dogs.

731
00:53:08,360 --> 00:53:11,360
I think we have corrupted dogs.

732
00:53:11,360 --> 00:53:14,360
Cats won't be corrupted.

733
00:53:14,360 --> 00:53:17,360
They simply won't be corrupted.

734
00:53:17,360 --> 00:53:18,860
And it seems to me a

735
00:53:18,860 --> 00:53:20,360
great irony that in the Middle Ages

736
00:53:20,360 --> 00:53:24,360
we should have burned cats along with witches.

737
00:53:24,360 --> 00:53:27,360
K. The ancient Egyptians worshipped cats.

738
00:53:27,360 --> 00:53:32,360
A. Yes. The great eye of the cat.

739
00:53:32,360 --> 00:53:36,360
I read some time ago that the cat's skeletal structure

740
00:53:36,360 --> 00:53:42,360
is among mammals the most perfectly adapted to its function.

741
00:53:42,360 --> 00:53:47,360
K. Correct.

742
00:53:47,360 --> 00:53:57,360
A. I think one of the most profound occasions

743
00:53:57,360 --> 00:54:00,360
for gratitude in my life

744
00:54:00,360 --> 00:54:03,360
was the living with a cat.

745
00:54:03,360 --> 00:54:11,360
And she taught me how to make an end.

746
00:54:11,360 --> 00:54:17,360
But I went through a lot of interior agony

747
00:54:17,360 --> 00:54:21,360
before I came to understand what she was doing.

748
00:54:21,360 --> 00:54:23,360
It's as though one would say of her

749
00:54:23,360 --> 00:54:33,360
that she was performing a mission, we might say,

750
00:54:33,360 --> 00:54:35,360
without, of course, being a missionary

751
00:54:35,360 --> 00:54:39,360
in the ordinary sense of the word.

752
00:54:39,360 --> 00:54:41,360
Yes, yes.

753
00:54:41,360 --> 00:54:47,360
K. Yes, sir. You see,

754
00:54:47,360 --> 00:54:54,360
one begins to see what freedom is in action.

755
00:54:54,360 --> 00:54:57,360
A. That's right.

756
00:54:57,360 --> 00:55:03,360
K. And the seeing in the doing

757
00:55:03,360 --> 00:55:07,360
is prevented by the observer

758
00:55:07,360 --> 00:55:15,360
who is the past, the formula, the concept, the belief.

759
00:55:16,360 --> 00:55:24,360
That observer comes in between perception and the doing.

760
00:55:24,360 --> 00:55:28,360
That observer is the factor of division,

761
00:55:28,360 --> 00:55:36,360
the idea and the conclusion in action.

762
00:55:37,360 --> 00:55:52,360
So, can we act only when there is perception?

763
00:55:53,360 --> 00:55:57,860
We do this, sir, when we

764
00:55:57,860 --> 00:56:02,360
are at the edge of a precipice.

765
00:56:02,360 --> 00:56:07,360
The seeing danger is instant action.

766
00:56:07,360 --> 00:56:09,360
A. If I remember correctly, the

767
00:56:09,360 --> 00:56:11,360
word alert comes from the Italian,

768
00:56:11,360 --> 00:56:15,360
which points to standing at the edge of a cliff.

769
00:56:15,360 --> 00:56:18,360
K. Cliff. That's right.

770
00:56:18,360 --> 00:56:21,360
A. That's pretty serious.

771
00:56:21,360 --> 00:56:25,360
K. You see, but it's very interesting.

772
00:56:25,360 --> 00:56:31,360
We are conditioned to danger of a cliff,

773
00:56:31,360 --> 00:56:35,360
of a snake, or a dangerous animal, and so on.

774
00:56:35,360 --> 00:56:37,360
We are conditioned.

775
00:56:37,360 --> 00:56:42,360
But we are not conditioned to...

776
00:56:42,360 --> 00:56:45,360
and we are conditioned also to this idea

777
00:56:45,360 --> 00:56:48,360
that you must act according to an idea,

778
00:56:48,360 --> 00:56:50,360
otherwise there is no action.

779
00:56:50,360 --> 00:56:52,360
A. No, we are conditioned to that.

780
00:56:52,360 --> 00:56:54,360
Terribly so.

781
00:56:54,360 --> 00:56:58,360
K. So we are conditioned to danger

782
00:56:58,360 --> 00:57:02,360
and conditioned to the fact

783
00:57:02,360 --> 00:57:06,360
that you cannot act without a formula,

784
00:57:06,360 --> 00:57:09,360
without a concept, belief, and so on.

785
00:57:09,360 --> 00:57:13,360
So these two are the factors of our conditioning.

786
00:57:13,360 --> 00:57:15,360
And now someone comes along and says,

787
00:57:15,360 --> 00:57:19,360
look, that's not action,

788
00:57:20,360 --> 00:57:24,360
that's merely a repetition of what has been,

789
00:57:24,360 --> 00:57:27,360
modified, but it's not action.

790
00:57:27,360 --> 00:57:31,360
Action is when you see and do.

791
00:57:31,360 --> 00:57:33,360
A. And the reaction to that is,

792
00:57:33,360 --> 00:57:35,360
oh, I see, he has a new definition of action.

793
00:57:35,360 --> 00:57:39,360
K. No, no, no. Yes. I'm not defining.

794
00:57:39,360 --> 00:57:40,360
A. Yes, of course not.

795
00:57:40,360 --> 00:57:42,360
K. I mean, I've done this all my life.

796
00:57:42,360 --> 00:57:46,360
I see something and I do it.

797
00:57:46,360 --> 00:57:49,360
Say, for instance, as you may know,

798
00:57:49,360 --> 00:57:53,360
I'm not being personal or anything,

799
00:57:53,360 --> 00:57:56,360
but a great big organisation,

800
00:57:56,360 --> 00:58:00,360
spiritual organisation with thousands of followers,

801
00:58:00,360 --> 00:58:02,360
with a great deal of land, five thousand acres,

802
00:58:02,360 --> 00:58:04,360
castles, and money, and so on,

803
00:58:04,360 --> 00:58:08,360
were formed around me, as a board.

804
00:58:08,360 --> 00:58:11,360
And in 1928 I said, this is all wrong,

805
00:58:11,360 --> 00:58:14,360
I dissolved it,

806
00:58:14,360 --> 00:58:17,360
returned the property and so on.

807
00:58:17,360 --> 00:58:20,360
I saw how wrong it was, the seeing,

808
00:58:20,360 --> 00:58:24,360
not the conclusions, comparisons,

809
00:58:24,360 --> 00:58:29,360
how religions have done, I saw and acted.

810
00:58:29,360 --> 00:58:30,360
A. Exactly.

811
00:58:30,360 --> 00:58:33,360
K. And therefore there has never been a regret.

812
00:58:33,360 --> 00:58:34,360
A. Marvellous.

813
00:58:34,360 --> 00:58:35,360
K. Never been said,

814
00:58:35,360 --> 00:58:38,360
oh, I made a mistake, sir,

815
00:58:38,360 --> 00:58:41,360
because I shall have nobody to lean on.

816
00:58:41,360 --> 00:58:43,360
You follow? A. Yes, I do.

817
00:58:43,360 --> 00:58:46,360
Could we next time, in our next conversation,

818
00:58:46,360 --> 00:58:48,360
relate beauty to seeing?

819
00:58:48,360 --> 00:58:49,360
That was splendid.

820
00:58:49,360 --> 00:58:52,360
K. Oh, splendid, yes, that's wonderful.

